From: DKR-R Win95 port
Subject: [PATCH] Count deposits into the guest queue, not the calls that ask

The trace in events.cpp counts calls to sp_complete. It cannot see what happens
here: a message that fails to land is requeued when its source allows retrying,
and this is the only place a duplicate deposit could be born.

It answered a different question than the one asked, which is the usual way an
instrument earns its keep. The scheduler's interrupt queue **saturates**: the
retrace message is deposited eight times -- OS_SC_MAX_MESGS -- and then refused.
Refusals are silent drops, retrace having no retry. Five messages were lost over
sixty frames. A game that counts retraces to pace itself will see time advance in
jerks, quite apart from the crash under investigation.

The per-message cap matters more than it looks. A single global cap let the most
frequent event eat the whole budget: retrace at sixty per second consumed forty
events before the SP edge arrived, and the log then read "the SP message is never
deposited" where it should have read "it has not been looked at yet". The cap is
now per message value, which keeps rare events visible when a frequent one
floods.

Off unless DKR_TRACE_SP is set.

diff --git a/ultramodern/src/mesgqueue.cpp b/ultramodern/src/mesgqueue.cpp
--- a/ultramodern/src/mesgqueue.cpp
+++ b/ultramodern/src/mesgqueue.cpp
@@ -1,3 +1,5 @@
+#include <cstdio>
+#include <cstdlib>
 #include <array>
 #include <bitset>
 #include <chrono>
@@ -174,6 +176,48 @@
     std::vector<QueuedMessage> requeued_messages{};
     while (external_messages.try_dequeue(to_send)) {
         const bool sent = do_send(PASS_RDRAM to_send.mq, to_send.mesg, to_send.jam, false);
+        // **Compter les depots, pas les demandes.**
+        //
+        // La trace de `events.cpp` compte les appels a `sp_complete`. Elle ne
+        // voit pas ce qui se passe ici : un message non delivre est remis en
+        // file quand sa source autorise le reessai, et c'est le seul endroit ou
+        // un depot en double pourrait naitre.
+        //
+        // Un plantage sur Windows 95 trouve `curRSPTask` nul dans
+        // `__scHandleRSP`, alors qu'une seule tache a ete soumise et un seul
+        // `sp_complete` appele. Si le compte ci-dessous depasse celui-la, la
+        // question est reglee.
+        if (std::getenv("DKR_TRACE_SP") != nullptr) {
+            static unsigned depots = 0, refus = 0, remises = 0;
+            if (sent) { depots++; } else if (to_send.requeue_if_blocked) { remises++; }
+            else { refus++; }
+            // **Un plafond global laisse le message le plus frequent devorer la
+            // trace.** La premiere version s'arretait apres quarante evenements,
+            // et le retrace — soixante par seconde — les avait tous consommes
+            // avant que le bord SP n'arrive. On aurait conclu que le message SP
+            // n'etait jamais depose, alors qu'il n'avait pas encore ete regarde.
+            //
+            // Le plafond est donc **par valeur de message** : les premieres
+            // occurrences de chacune, ce qui garde les rares visibles.
+            static unsigned vus[8][2];
+            static unsigned nb_vus = 0;
+            unsigned k = 0, compte = 0;
+            const unsigned val = (unsigned)(uintptr_t)to_send.mesg;
+            for (k = 0; k < nb_vus; k++) {
+                if (vus[k][0] == val) { compte = ++vus[k][1]; break; }
+            }
+            if (k == nb_vus && nb_vus < 8u) {
+                vus[nb_vus][0] = val; vus[nb_vus][1] = 1; nb_vus++; compte = 1;
+            }
+            if (compte <= 4u) {
+                std::fprintf(stderr,
+                             "[trace][mq] mq=0x%08X msg=0x%08X %s"
+                             "  depots=%u remises=%u refus=%u\n",
+                             (unsigned)to_send.mq, (unsigned)(uintptr_t)to_send.mesg,
+                             sent ? "depose" : (to_send.requeue_if_blocked ? "remis" : "refuse"),
+                             depots, remises, refus);
+            }
+        }
         if (!sent && to_send.requeue_if_blocked) {
             requeued_messages.push_back(to_send);
         } else if (sent) {
